One subtlety made my first attempt useless: `reset_pending` replaces the whole cell with a PENDING marker at the start of every run, destroying the prior verdict before `record()` sees it — so history only ever captured `"PENDING"`. The push has to happen there too.

## It immediately caught two real defects at 32/caw

Both are new, both genuine, both now in the ledger:

- **`dir_reuse_coherency`** — `checks=51 passed=50 failed=1` with **all 32 nodes agreeing on the wrong answer**. That's a coherent durable wrong result, not a per-node flake. Re-ran green (58/58), so without the history it would have been erased. Also: the failing run did **fewer** checks (51 vs 58) — that may be the clue.
- **`crash_consistency`** — `NO_TERMINAL_RECORD` on all 32 nodes at exactly **90s/90s**. It passes at 16 nodes in 32s, and the reconvergence gate confirmed the cluster was healthy afterwards, so the *test* didn't finish. RULE 0 says don't widen the budget — instrument where it stalls.

The open-defect count went **up, 6 → 8**. That's the honest direction: the board got better at seeing, so it saw more. Still not production ready; marker not written.
Criteria not met — 8 open defects. Next: make `D-DIR-REUSE-COHERENCY-32-FLAKY` diagnosable. Its blocking fix is that the node script must **name** the failing check. Let me see if that can be fixed generically for all criteria.
`finish()` already names the failing check in its `reason` — the loss happens in **`run.sh`'s aggregation**, which reports counts from a *passing* node and discards the failing node's reason. That's the same weakness state.md flagged. Let me fix it generically.
At the relay boundary — let me land this one fix cleanly.
Fix landed and verified (syntax clean, criterion still runs green so the change is inert on a passing run — it only engages on a failure). Stopping here at the relay boundary.

## This session

**Criteria not met — 8 open defects. Marker not written.**

**Root-caused and fixed one thing I broke:** my `igrab`/`iput` macro wrapper dereferenced before the NULL check. `iput(NULL)` is legal and MXFS relies on it, so every node panicked at mount in `mxfs_dlm_pr_sweep_work_fn` (fault address `0x33c` = `XFS_I(NULL)` + field offset). The reboots cost `/src`, which made prep power-cycle, which cost `/src` again — a loop that presented as a failing rig, not a bad build. Fixed in 0.11.193; `scripts/rig_recover.sh` added to break that loop.

**Two measurements that invalidate the previous model of the critical defect:**
- P195 fires on *passing* runs (2 of 16 nodes) and was *absent* from a run that lost 3 entries. So it is neither necessary nor sufficient — `dirent_publish_integrity`'s justification as "the deterministic predicate" is measurably wrong.
- `P32E-DIREPOCH-FENCE` is the **only** marker that is zero on every passing run measured, while appearing in every captured loss chain. That's the target, and 0.11.194 adds `racebail_age_ms` to it so one captured failure settles whether the untouched `P34J-RELOAD-RACE-BAIL` (105–108/run, the twin sess22 didn't fix) is the second producer.

**Harness now tells the truth in four ways it didn't:** unrun work is `NOT_RUN`/`ABORTED` not FAIL; a destructive criterion owns its reconvergence postcondition and blocks rather than poisons what follows (`crash_consistency` went 9/16 → 16/16); `kernel_health` no longer fires on a firmware boot line; and cells carry flake history, so a green cell with a recent failure renders `⚠ FLAKY [N of last M runs FAILED]`.

**The ledger went 6 → 8 open** — the board got better at seeing, so it saw two more real defects at 32/caw: `dir_reuse_coherency` failing one check with all 32 nodes agreeing on the wrong answer (caught only because of the new flake history), and `crash_consistency` blowing its 90s budget at 32 nodes having passed in 32s at 16.

Rig is healthy and mounted at 32/caw on 0.11.194.

